The King’s Imaginary Seat

A court comedy in three acts for numbers, musicians, and one empty chair.

The cast. Two seats everybody in pairs. Three keeps asking for a solo. Four runs the palace and would like everyone to use the same door. One is the king. The court musician, i, turns any company a quarter turn counterclockwise. The musician’s twin, −i, turns it clockwise.

The stage is a cross. Stage right is positive real. Upstage is positive imaginary. A brass peg marks zero. The queen’s chair sits two paces right of the peg and three upstage. A cord runs from the peg to the chair.

Act I · Four Brings a Plus-One

On the anniversary of the queen’s death, King One had the orchestra play the piece she had been writing when she died. He instructed them to stop at the blank bar.

Four had prepared a grander entrance.

FOUR: At the queen’s last written note, we move her chair to the new royal box. Eleven right, ten upstage. The court will rise.

TWO: How are we getting there?

Four handed him a slip marked 4 − i.

TWO: Is that an instruction or a complaint?

FOUR: It’s my whole department.

Two called for four copies of the original position. Eight paces right, twelve upstage. Then the musician’s twin turned one original copy clockwise. Its three upstage paces swung to the right. Its two rightward paces swung downstage. Add that displacement and the chair would land eleven right and ten upstage.

Three refused to do it that way. She had three musicians and wanted to give each a part.

She sent the first to multiply the two real counts, 2 × 4. The second multiplied the two imaginary counts, 3 × (−1). The third added the counts in each company first, then multiplied the totals, 5 × 3.

The three musicians called back 8, −3, and 15.

THREE: Fifteen includes every pairing. Take out the eight and the minus three. What’s left goes upstage.

MUSICIAN: Taking out a minus three gives us three back?

THREE: Tell the treasurer before he gets home.

Ten upstage. Rightward, the two imaginary units paired into a minus sign, so the negative three became positive three. Eleven right.

The two processions met at the new box. Two had brought a chair. Three had brought a marching band.

The king looked from the box to the place beside the old brass peg.

ONE: Put it back.

FOUR: I’ve already had the plaque engraved.

ONE: Then you know where to find the plaque.

Act II · The Two Queens

In the commotion, a page had untied the original cord to hang a banner. Four could remember how far the old chair had been from the peg. The square of the distance was thirteen. Two remembered that it had been two paces right. Nobody standing near the king could agree which side of the real axis the platform had called upstage.

Four sent a page to fetch chalk. Three called the twins.

Three’s route. She asked i and −i for a way to undo the move. The twins stood beside Four in turn, making 4 − i and 4 + i. Together those two instructions canceled the sideways turns and gave a plain scale of 17.

Three applied 4 + i to the chair’s new position. The resulting directions were 34 right, 51 upstage. Seventeen divided each count into equal shares.

THREE: Two right. Three upstage.

SEVENTEEN: Am I dismissed?

THREE: How long have you been waiting?

SEVENTEEN: Since Act One. There were seventeen of me in the dressing room and one chair.

Two’s route. He drew a circle around the peg using the old distance, then walked two paces right and drew a line across the stage. The line crossed the circle twice. He laid a chair at each crossing.

TWO: Two squared is four. Thirteen less four leaves nine. Three paces either way.

FOUR: Pick one.

TWO: I did. Then I checked.

Three was already sitting in the upstage chair. Two turned her chalk line toward the king so he could see the other seat.

TWO: Did Her Majesty ever sit down here, sir?

One walked downstage. The seat faced the orchestra at an angle he had never seen from his box. You could watch the percussionist’s hands from there. He sat a while.

ONE: Here’s where she used to go when she wanted to hear what everybody else was playing.

He asked them to leave both chairs.

Act III · The Wrong Note

The orchestra took up the queen’s unfinished piece. One had ordered the conductor to stop at the blank bar, and for years she had obeyed. Tonight, when they reached it, the conductor lifted her hands. The musicians lowered their bows.

From the downstage chair, One could see the percussionist take his thumb off the little bronze bell.

ONE: What were you about to do?

PERCUSSIONIST: She used to turn her hand at the end, sir. I always thought it meant my bell was too loud.

From the upstage chair, Three looked across at the percussionist. She held her hand the way the queen had held hers in the portrait over the musicians’ door. Then she turned it a quarter turn.

The court musician i saw the gesture. He turned the upstage chair’s position through a quarter turn around the peg, from (2, 3) to (−3, 2). The percussionist looked down. Three left, two upstage was exactly where he stood.

The musician turned the position again. (−2, −3). The violist stood there. She lifted her bow.

Once more. (3, −2). A child held the smallest horn in the orchestra. She put it to her lips and blew a note so large her feet slipped forward on the bench.

The king laughed. The child lowered the horn, mortified.

ONE: Keep that one.

On the fourth turn, the cue came back to (2, 3). Three stood by the empty chair. All her life she’d wanted the room to go quiet so she could sing alone.

She held the silence for a beat. Then she turned her palm out toward the orchestra.

From the other chair, the king could see each player look up in turn. He heard the bell, then the viola, then the far-too-large horn. The conductor raised her hands and the rest of the orchestra came in around them. Four took off his ceremonial gloves so he could clap properly when they finished.

The court copyist bent over the blank bar.

COPYIST: What shall I put here, sir?

One listened until he could hear the horn player laughing in the silence after the last note.

ONE: Put a repeat sign.

